(ns inga.quorum)

;; Every number that answers "is this a quorum", in Kotoba.
;;
;; `inga.quorum` is a namespace of PREDICATES, and a predicate is a closure --
;; `at-least` returns a function, `stake-weighted` returns a function carrying
;; a profile in its metadata, `->predicate` dispatches on the runtime type of
;; its argument. None of that crosses, and none of it is the decision. The
;; decision is the arithmetic inside: a threshold derived from n, and a
;; comparison of an already-resolved count or sum against it.
;;
;; That arithmetic is spread over three namespaces -- `inga.consensus` owns
;; `quorum-size`/`byzantine-tolerance`, `inga.quorum` owns `one-honest` and
;; what `at-least`'s closure does, `inga.stake` owns the >2/3-of-stake rule
;; and its unbonded head-count fallback -- and it is one subject. A replica
;; that computes a different threshold than its peers does not produce a
;; wrong number somebody notices; it produces two certificates at one height,
;; or a certificate nobody else will accept, and either way the safety lemma
;; the whole system rests on is gone. Same argument `kotoba/fuel.kotoba`
;; makes for the metering arithmetic, applied to the other number a replica
;; cannot be alone in believing.
;;
;; ## What is NOT here, deliberately
;;
;; Counting the witnesses, summing the bonds, deduplicating votes by witness,
;; verifying signatures, attaching a profile to a closure, and choosing which
;; predicate to hand a caller all stay in the cljc. They are folds over
;; collections and dispatches on runtime types, not decisions -- and
;; `stake-quorum-met?` reduces to a two-integer comparison the moment its two
;; sums exist. `total-stake` produces the sums; `stake-met` below decides.
;;
;; ## This module is the reference. It is NOT what production executes.
;;
;; `inga.consensus` / `inga.quorum` / `inga.stake` decide; the cljs suite runs
;; the checked-in `quorum.wasm` against them over a matrix. Superproject
;; ADR-2608112100 calls that arrangement a first step and the workspace pattern
;; is to finish it by having the host execute the shipped artifact. inga was
;; measured against that standard and deliberately stopped here.
;;
;; The short version, because a reader of this file should not have to find the
;; README to know whether it runs: a compiled module traps when its own fuel
;; runs out (256 calls of `quorum-size` on one instance, measured), and
;; exhaustion-as-a-trap is the one thing `inga.fuel` forbids; while delegating
;; to a KIR interpreter would make the threshold a function of which
;; interpreter pin a replica has, which is precisely the disagreement the
;; paragraph above says costs a fork. README, 'What the Kotoba cores actually
;; run', has the measurements.
;;
;; What that leaves is real and is the point: the numbers below are the
;; reference, the cljc is the implementation, and if they ever disagree the
;; answer here is the correct one. `inga.kotoba-reproducibility-test` keeps the
;; checked-in binary equal to this source compiled.
;;
;; ## Pure i64 by construction
;;
;; No capability is declared because none is needed. Every function here takes
;; i64 and returns i64, uses only `+ - * quot` and comparison, and calls only
;; other functions in this module -- which is exactly the word-typed slice
;; `kotoba.kir/only-native-word-typed-features?` admits, so this module is
;; native-AOT-qualifiable as well as wasm32.
;;
;; ## Predicates return 1/0, not :bool
;;
;; Following `fuel.kotoba`'s `fits`, and not merely for consistency: every
;; comparison in this frontend infers as `:i64`, never `:bool` (a literal
;; `true`/`false` is the only way to obtain a genuine `:bool` value at all --
;; see the comment on `only-native-word-typed-features?` in kotoba-kir). A
;; `:bool`-returning predicate would therefore have to wrap the comparison it
;; already computed, and the wasm export would still hand JavaScript a number.

;; ── thresholds derived from n ───────────────────────────────────────────────

;; `inga.consensus/byzantine-tolerance`: f for a given n, where n = 3f+1.
;;
;; `quot` truncates toward zero on both sides -- Clojure's `quot` by
;; definition, wasm's `i64.div_s` by the spec -- so the two agree on n = 0
;; (`(quot -1 3)` is 0, not -1) without either being told to.
(defn byzantine-tolerance [n :i64] :i64
  (quot (- n 1) 3))

;; `inga.consensus/quorum-size`: the smallest quorum that is SAFE for n, i.e.
;; the smallest size at which any two quorums must share an honest witness.
;;
;; Stated as ceil((n + f + 1) / 2) rather than 2f+1, because 2f+1 is only
;; equal to it ON the n = 3f+1 grid and is silently unsafe off it: at n = 6,
;; f = 1, 2f+1 = 3 admits two DISJOINT quorums, which is two conflicting
;; certificates at one height. The cljc computes the ceiling as
;; `(quot (+ n f 2) 2)`; the same integer division is written out here.
;;
;;     n=4 -> 3    n=5 -> 4    n=6 -> 4    n=7 -> 5    n=10 -> 7
(defn quorum-size [n :i64] :i64
  (quot (+ (+ n (byzantine-tolerance n)) 2) 2))

;; `inga.quorum/one-honest`: f+1, the smallest number of DISTINCT witnesses
;; that must contain at least one honest one.
;;
;; Not a quorum and used for a different job: a quorum decides what is agreed,
;; this decides what is BELIEVABLE. Head count only -- the stake-weighted
;; analogue is deliberately unimplemented in the cljc and is not invented here.
(defn one-honest [n :i64] :i64
  (+ (byzantine-tolerance n) 1))

;; ── head count ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

;; The body of the closure `inga.quorum/at-least` returns: at least `threshold`
;; distinct witnesses voted. 1 = quorum, 0 = not.
;;
;; `votes` is the count of DISTINCT witnesses. Distinctness is the caller's --
;; `inga.consensus/qc` takes `(set (map :inga.vote/witness votes))` before it
;; ever reaches a threshold -- and that dedup is the place equivocation is
;; neutralised, so it is worth naming what this function is trusting.
(defn at-least-met [votes :i64 threshold :i64] :i64
  (if (>= votes threshold) 1 0))

;; The body of the closure `inga.quorum/for-set-size` returns. Takes n, the
;; size of the SET, and derives the threshold -- which is the whole reason the
;; cljc names it apart from `at-least`: `(at-least 4)` requires four votes and
;; `(for-set-size 4)` requires three, and the two read identically at a call
;; site if the number is allowed to mean either.
(defn for-set-size-met [votes :i64 n :i64] :i64
  (at-least-met votes (quorum-size n)))

;; ── stake weight ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

;; The decision inside `inga.stake/stake-quorum-met?`, once `total-stake` has
;; produced its two sums: more than two thirds of the total bonded stake.
;;
;; `(> (* 3 voted) (* 2 total))` and not `(> (/ voted total) (2/3))`: the
;; cross-multiplied form is exact in integers, has no rounding to disagree
;; about between replicas, and is what the cljc writes. STRICTLY greater --
;; exactly two thirds is not a quorum, which is what makes two quorums
;; intersect in more than a third of the stake.
;;
;; Zero total is 0 and not 1. The cljc's `(and (pos? total) ...)` guard is not
;; a special case bolted on: with no stake at all, `(> 0 0)` is false anyway,
;; but `(> (* 3 0) (* 2 0))` would also be false for a FULL set, so without
;; the guard the answer would be "no quorum" for a reason that has nothing to
;; do with who voted. `inga.stake/quorum-met?` reads this branch and falls
;; back -- see `stake-basis` / `counted-unbonded-met`.
(defn stake-met [voted-stake :i64 total-stake :i64] :i64
  (if (> total-stake 0)
    (if (> (* 3 voted-stake) (* 2 total-stake)) 1 0)
    0))

;; Which security basis `inga.stake/quorum-met?` is operating under.
;; 1 = :stake-weighted (Sybil-resistant), 0 = :counted-unbonded (NOT).
;;
;; This is a decision and not a label. The cljc returns it in-band precisely
;; so a caller cannot read `:met? true` and believe it got Byzantine security
;; when nobody has bonded anything; a replica that took the other branch than
;; its peers would be applying a different rule under the same name.
(defn stake-basis [total-stake :i64] :i64
  (if (> total-stake 0) 1 0))

;; `inga.stake/quorum-met?`'s fallback branch: more than two thirds of the
;; enumerated roster BY HEAD when no collateral is bonded.
;;
;; Same cross-multiplied comparison as `stake-met`, over counts instead of
;; sums, with the same strictness. It is a liveness arrangement and not BFT --
;; anyone can mint did:keys for free -- and it is ported because it is a rule
;; replicas must agree on, not because it is a good one.
(defn counted-unbonded-met [n-voted :i64 n :i64] :i64
  (if (> n 0)
    (if (> (* 3 n-voted) (* 2 n)) 1 0)
    0))

;; The composite `inga.stake/quorum-met?` answers with, given all four
;; already-resolved numbers: whichever branch `stake-basis` selects.
(defn quorum-met [voted-stake :i64 total-stake :i64 n-voted :i64 n :i64] :i64
  (if (> total-stake 0)
    (stake-met voted-stake total-stake)
    (counted-unbonded-met n-voted n)))

;; n = 4 is the smallest set with a nonzero f, so this is the smallest input
;; on which quorum-size is not simply "everyone".
(defn main [] :i64
  (quorum-size 4))
